<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Draa River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Wadi Draa, Oued Draa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Draa is the longest river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a> and flowing south-east and then west to the Atlantic, though its lower course is usually dry. Its middle valley is a long chain of palm oases and fortified villages on the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. The Saadi family, who ruled Morocco in the 16th and 17th centuries, came from this valley.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/draa-river.jpg" alt="Draa River" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jon Lean from Bristol, United Kingdom · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Draa_River.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
